## The river at the gentle end of the day.

Aswan is lovely from the water, and lovelier in the afternoon. The light softens, the granite of the islands turns from grey to bronze, and the dunes of the west bank glow above the palms. A motorboat of your own heads south, past Elephantine and the botanical island, in among the boulders of the first cataract, where the Nile once broke into rapids at the edge of ancient Egypt.

Then, on the west bank, a village of painted houses comes into view, climbing up from the water.

## Gharb Soheil

The Nubians are the people of the Nile south of Aswan, with their own languages, their own music and their own way of building: domed roofs, vaulted rooms that stay cool through the summer, walls washed white and blue and ochre and painted with stars, crescents and geometric patterns. Gharb Soheil is one of the villages that keep that tradition alive, its houses bright against the desert.

Some of the village has turned towards visitors, with stalls along the landing and houses that keep crocodiles in cages for photographs. We walk past all of that, up the sandy lanes, to the home of a family we know.

> You come as a guest, with an introduction, and the tea is already poured.

## Tea, and a conversation

You sit in a cool, high-ceilinged room or up on the roof terrace with a glass of hibiscus or mint tea. There is time to ask about Nubian life, about the language, about the villages further south that were lost when Lake Nasser rose behind the High Dam and their families resettled. On your Signature day, a Nubian meal at the family's table can be arranged ahead.

## Home on golden water

Then down to the river again, the boat waiting, and the long, slow run back to Aswan as the sun goes down: feluccas out on the water, the Aga Khan's mausoleum pale on its hill, the town lighting up ahead. The boat, the family, the timing: your concierge takes care of it all, with local knowledge and our craft, so the only thing you have to decide is when to leave.
